# Kaizen Design System — Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is **kaizen** — a Vue 3 component library built with TypeScript, Vite, and Storybook (`@storybook/vue3-vite`).
It is NOT a React project. All components are Vue 3 Single File Components (SFC).

Figma source file: **Kaizen Reworked 3 Lvls**
Figma library & tokens: https://www.figma.com/design/JCQ4u9ytPIMpGaLzdAq8dD/Kaizen-Reworked-3-Lvls?node-id=1930-5660&p=f&t=DUBWp0ybbtNgIppz-0

---

## Token Architecture (3-Level System)

The design system uses a strict 3-level token architecture. **Always respect these rules:**

| Collection | Role | Use in components? |
|---|---|---|
| `core-primitive` | Raw values (colors, spacing base). Never assigned to roles. | ❌ NEVER |
| `core-alias` | Brand decisions and role assignments | ❌ NEVER directly |
| `core-mapped` | Component-level decisions. Final token mapping. | ✅ ALWAYS use these |
| `system-fixed` | Values identical across all brands | ✅ Use when applicable |
| `system-responsive` | Breakpoint/screen-width dependent values | ✅ Use when applicable |

**Rule:** Components always consume `core-mapped` and `system-*` tokens. Never use `core-primitive` or `core-alias` directly in component CSS.

---

## CSS Namespace

All CSS classes use the `ds-kaizen` namespace prefix:

```css
.ds-kaizen-c-badge { }
.ds-kaizen-c-badge--large { }
.ds-kaizen-c-badge__icon { }
```

Pattern: `ds-kaizen-c-<component-name>` for block, `--<modifier>` for modifiers, `__<element>` for elements (BEM).

---

## Component File Structure

Every component lives in `src/components/<ComponentName>/` and contains exactly these files:

```
src/components/Badge/
├── Badge.vue              # Vue 3 SFC implementation
├── Badge.module.css       # BEM CSS with ds-kaizen namespace + core-mapped tokens
├── Badge.stories.ts       # Storybook stories
└── README.md              # Documentation (follow CLAUDE.md template)
```

---

## How to Build a Component

### Step 1 — Read Figma
Use Figma MCP to read the component node from Figma. Extract:
- All variants and props
- All states (default, hover, focus, disabled, etc.)
- Exact token names used (must be from `core-mapped` or `system-*`)
- Spacing, typography, border-radius values
- Accessibility requirements (focus ring, ARIA roles, keyboard nav)

### Step 2 — Badge.vue (Vue 3 SFC)
```vue
<template>
  <span
    :class="[
      $style['ds-kaizen-c-badge'],
      $style[`ds-kaizen-c-badge--${variant}`],
      $style[`ds-kaizen-c-badge--${size}`],
      { [$style['ds-kaizen-c-badge--rounded']]: rounded }
    ]"
    v-bind="$attrs"
  >
    <slot name="iconLeft" />
    <slot />
    <slot name="iconRight" />
  </span>
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
defineOptions({ inheritAttrs: false })

withDefaults(defineProps<{
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'success' | 'warning' | 'error' | 'neutral'
  size?: 'small' | 'large'
  rounded?: boolean
}>(), {
  variant: 'neutral',
  size: 'small',
  rounded: false
})
</script>

<style module src="./Badge.module.css" />
```

### Step 3 — Badge.module.css
- Use CSS custom properties from `core-mapped` tokens
- Use BEM with `ds-kaizen` namespace
- Always include focus-visible ring for interactive elements
- Never hardcode color values — always use token variables

```css
.ds-kaizen-c-badge {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--core-mapped-badge-gap);
  padding: var(--core-mapped-badge-padding-y) var(--core-mapped-badge-padding-x);
  border-radius: var(--core-mapped-badge-border-radius);
  font-size: var(--system-fixed-badge-font-size);
  font-weight: var(--system-fixed-badge-font-weight);
  color: var(--core-mapped-badge-text-default);
  background-color: var(--core-mapped-badge-surface-default);
}

/* Always include focus ring for accessibility */
.ds-kaizen-c-badge:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--core-mapped-focus-ring);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

### Step 4 — Badge.stories.ts
```typescript
import type { Meta, StoryObj } from '@storybook/vue3'
import Badge from './Badge.vue'

const meta: Meta<typeof Badge> = {
  title: 'Components/Badge',
  component: Badge,
  tags: ['autodocs'],
  argTypes: {
    variant: {
      control: 'select',
      options: ['primary', 'secondary', 'success', 'warning', 'error', 'neutral']
    },
    size: {
      control: 'radio',
      options: ['small', 'large']
    },
    rounded: { control: 'boolean' }
  }
}

export default meta
type Story = StoryObj<typeof meta>

export const Default: Story = {
  args: { variant: 'neutral', size: 'small', rounded: false }
}

export const AllVariants: Story = {
  render: () => ({
    components: { Badge },
    template: `
      <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <Badge variant="primary">Primary</Badge>
        <Badge variant="secondary">Secondary</Badge>
        <Badge variant="success">Success</Badge>
        <Badge variant="warning">Warning</Badge>
        <Badge variant="error">Error</Badge>
        <Badge variant="neutral">Neutral</Badge>
      </div>
    `
  })
}
```

### Step 5 — README.md
Follow the Component Documentation Template defined in `CLAUDE.md`. Always include:
- Overview
- Figma link + node ID
- Props table (name, type, default, description)
- Slots table
- Events table
- Usage examples
- Accessibility notes (focus ring, ARIA, keyboard)
- Token mapping table (prop/state → core-mapped token)
- Storybook notes

---

## Accessibility Rules (Always Apply)

- Every interactive element must have `:focus-visible` with `var(--core-mapped-focus-ring)`
- Use semantic HTML elements where possible
- Add ARIA roles/attributes when semantic HTML is insufficient
- Keyboard navigation must work for all interactive components
- Color contrast must meet WCAG AA minimum (4.5:1 for text)
- Never use color alone to convey meaning — always pair with icon or text

---

## Workflow for Each New Component

1. **Get Figma link** for the component node
2. **Use Figma MCP** to extract design data (variants, tokens, states)
3. **Delete old version** of the component if it exists
4. **Create** `ComponentName.vue`, `ComponentName.module.css`, `ComponentName.stories.ts`, `README.md`
5. **Run** `npm run storybook` to verify it renders correctly
6. **Never** use `core-primitive` or `core-alias` tokens in component CSS

---

## Theme Switcher

The Storybook theme switcher reads from the `core-mapped` collection which has `dark` and `light` modes. The `data-theme` attribute on the root element controls which mode is active. This is already configured in `.storybook/preview.ts`.

---

## What NOT to Do

- ❌ Never create React components (.tsx, .jsx)
- ❌ Never use `core-primitive` or `core-alias` tokens directly in component CSS
- ❌ Never hardcode color hex values in CSS
- ❌ Never skip README.md documentation
- ❌ Never skip focus-visible styles on interactive elements
- ❌ Never use inline styles for design token values
